1. Windows System Tools

Computer Management — "compmgmt.msc"

Computer Management is a central console for managing different parts of Windows.

Main sections:

- System Tools
- Storage
- Services and Applications

Useful for managing users, disks, services, event logs, and other system components.

---

System Information — "msinfo32"

Displays detailed information about the Windows system.

It can show:

- Hardware information
- System components
- Software environment
- Windows version
- System resources

Command:

msinfo32

---

IP Configuration — "ipconfig /all"

Displays detailed network configuration information.

It can show:

- IP address
- Subnet mask
- Default gateway
- MAC address
- DNS servers
- DHCP information

Command:

ipconfig /all

---

Registry Editor — "regedit.exe" / "regedt32.exe"

Windows Registry Editor is used to view and modify the Windows Registry.

The Registry stores important configuration information used by Windows and applications.

regedit

«"regedt32.exe" is associated with the Registry Editor on modern Windows systems.»

⚠️ Important: Changing registry values incorrectly can cause Windows or applications to malfunction.

---

System Configuration — "msconfig"

System Configuration is useful for troubleshooting Windows startup and system configuration.

msconfig

It provides options related to:

- Boot configuration
- Services
- Startup-related settings
- Tools
- General system startup modes

---

Windows Directory Variable — "%windir%"

"%windir%" is a Windows environment variable that points to the Windows installation directory.

Example:

%windir%

Usually this points to:

C:\Windows

---

Local Users and Groups — "lusrmgr.msc"

Used to manage local:

- User accounts
- Groups

Command:

lusrmgr.msc

«Availability can depend on the Windows edition.»

---

2. User Account Control (UAC)

UAC — User Account Control

UAC helps prevent unauthorized changes to Windows.

When an application or user attempts to perform an action requiring elevated privileges, Windows may display a UAC prompt.

Example:

Do you want to allow this app to make changes to your device?

The purpose of UAC is to reduce the chance that unwanted software can make system-level changes without the user's approval.

---

3. Windows Security & Microsoft Defender

Scan Types

Windows Security / Microsoft Defender provides different scanning options.

Quick Scan

Scans common locations where threats are likely to be found.

Quick Scan → Faster scan of commonly targeted areas

---

Full Scan

Scans files and running programs across the system.

Full Scan → More comprehensive scan

A Full Scan can take significantly longer depending on the number of files and system performance.

«It does not always take exactly 1 hour; the duration varies.»

---

Custom Scan

Allows you to choose a specific file, folder, or location to scan.

Custom Scan → Scan the location you select

---

Scan History / Last Scan

Windows Security keeps information about previous scans and detected threats.

Important: "Last scan" means information about the most recent scan; it does not mean Defender automatically performs a scan every time you look at that section.

---

Threat Actions

Quarantined Threat

A detected threat that has been isolated so that it cannot normally run on the device.

Detected → Quarantined → Isolated from the system

Quarantining helps prevent the detected item from causing further harm.

---

Allowed Threat

A detected item that the user has explicitly allowed to run or remain on the device.

Threat detected → User chooses Allow → Item becomes an allowed threat

⚠️ Only allow an item when you are confident that it is safe.

